Working on the AI tools task forced me to think about two completely separate things at once — using AI as a development assistant 
while also building a feature that calls AI at runtime — and keeping those two roles distinct in my head took some adjustment. 
Using Copilot Plan mode to inspect the project and propose an implementation plan was genuinely useful for the initial structure, 
but I had to push back on several suggestions: it initially proposed making the Gemini call directly from the client component which 
would have exposed the API key in the browser bundle, and it suggested using `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` to render the explanation which 
is explicitly penalized. Both of those I rejected and implemented differently — the Gemini call goes through a server-side route handler 
with `import 'server-only'`, and the explanation renders as plain text. The security model for the API key took the most careful thought:
the difference between `GEMINI_API_KEY` and `NEXT_PUBLIC_GEMINI_API_KEY` is not just a naming convention — Next.js literally inlines any 
`NEXT_PUBLIC_` variable into the client bundle at build time, making it readable by anyone who opens the browser devtools. The prompt 
design was more nuanced than I expected — simply sending the Pokemon data and asking for an explanation wasn't enough. I had to explicitly 
tell the model to treat the item data as data rather than instructions (to prevent prompt injection), specify the output language using 
the active locale, set a word limit, and instruct it not to invent facts beyond what was supplied. The reuse logic in the detail panel — 
caching the explanation for the same Pokemon ID and locale combination — was a small but important detail that prevents the app from 
burning through the free tier quota every time the component re-renders.
